+# Task10 fix01 implementation report
+
+Author scope: I1 + I2 from independent review `task-10-independent-review-20261024.md` (SHA256 `d6483b6d15c431b68ba2fa01623358e719cac2a6da8247718ad0751bdc500103`). Fixed comparison base `5657d24a4a58916dfca14a29e352ad361dda3319`. Author status: implemented, focused verification completed, awaiting the same reviewer's scoped verdict. This is not Task10 adoption, formal Task11 activation or final Phase B acceptance. Work crossed midnight into 2026-10-25 Asia/Shanghai; retained filenames identify the existing review round and actual command timestamps are unchanged.
+
+## I1 — ordinary deferred failure protocol
+
+`MinutesDeferredPreparation.java:9–17` is the common check used by all six final receipt branches in saved/adopt/body/set/restore, review, settings and decision commands. Initial authenticated accepted lookup still returns first. After a miss, the final transaction claims the receipt, acquires fresh authorization and matches the request digest; the new check then propagates any observed nonordinary error before a concurrent winner may be restored. Ordinary new-command errors still yield to that winner before revision/config checks. No second issuer, no new capability and no changes to digest/auth precedence.
+
+Ordinary errors are explicitly named: bounded budget exhaustion, actual `ARCHIVE_IO_UNAVAILABLE`, and the listed revision/current-target/editor-intent/business-readiness/sequence conflicts. Generic `PROFILE_UNAVAILABLE`, `PROVENANCE_UNAVAILABLE`, `CAPABILITY_UNAVAILABLE`, fingerprint failures, access denial, programming exceptions and cancellation do not qualify merely because they represent 503/unavailability. Cancellation/interruption remains nonordinary.
+
+`ControlledConfigArchiveSource.java:15–18,27,44–47,56–60,89–95` distinguishes actual filesystem acquisition IO from integrity. Only protected file/directory open/read/list calls are inside the acquisition wrapper. Custom JSON parsing, SHA checks, length/shape assertions, reference/dependency validation and binding occur outside its IOException catch; existing `Failure` thrown inside a protected read also passes through unchanged. `InterruptedIOException`, `ClosedByInterruptException`, cancellation and thread interrupt do not become availability. Resource-close/security/unsupported-filesystem and iterator errors retain the original nonordinary failure. No paths, causes, secrets or bytes enter the safe exception. Existing HTTP advice still maps the new code to its existing unavailable response.
+
+The actual concurrent test first observes no receipt, pauses preparation, lets another thread commit the same command key, and then raises the captured failure. The three saved/review/settings families use real missing-archive acquisition and a real index-SHA mismatch, plus precise programming/provenance injections. All ordinary cases restore the winner; each nonordinary case propagates the identical error and leaves the winner's root, graph/history, receipts, audit and outbox snapshot unchanged. A separate real candidate reject tests the decision caller's same miss/winner/programming path. It is not an initial-hit replay test.
+
+Four existing classifiers additionally accept the new named IO code to preserve their prior actual filesystem-availability behavior: `JdbcSavedTranscripts.ordinaryPreparationFailure`, `JdbcMinutesInitialSettings.ordinary`, `JdbcMinutesGenerationCommands.ordinary`, and the `JdbcProcessingIntake` MinutesContracts.Failure catch. `legacy-io-final-01` exercises all four actual command branches with genuine initial misses, concurrent accepted winners and actual missing archive IO; exact post-winner snapshots remain unchanged. The generation is only locally claimed/completed FAILED (no external marker/POST); the import is processed to SUCCEEDED by its real worker.
+
+The old four classifiers still treat mixed `PROFILE_UNAVAILABLE` as ordinary. A real SHA/schema/binding failure after their initial miss concurrent with a winner may therefore have an I1-like concealment risk. This unchanged policy is explicitly OPEN for final source review, separately from Task9 F5. This wave adds only the precisely named IO compatibility case and does not claim those legacy integrity branches repaired.
+
+## I2 — complete bounded prospective accounting
+
+`MinutesDomainCapacity.java:14–37` accounts by primary key for these row families: generation_input, durable_task, task_attempt, external_operation, external_execution and generation_candidate. It merges current source discovery and shared minutes declaration without counting the same row twice. Existing bounded SQL selectors retain their sentinel `LIMIT 257` and reject overflow. All matching meeting `TRANSCRIPT_IMPORT_V1` and `MINUTES_GENERATE_V1` history is retained, including terminal tasks, plus explicitly merged source task identities. This is deliberately more complete than the previous current-source-only task union. No history is deleted, hidden or truncated; pre-existing legacy overflow remains failclosed. The meeting subject predicate uses the existing indexed FK subject prefix; no SQL/schema/index is changed.
+
+The existing independent limits remain independent: durable tasks <= min(256, registered tasks); generation inputs <= min(256, registered revisionNodes); attempts, operations, executions and candidate declarations <= 256. These are the existing consumer capacities, not new product quotas. In particular tasks=2 is not reinterpreted as an attempt limit of 2.
+
+For each family, prospective size is distinct occupied rows plus still-possible growth:
+
+- task_attempt: actual retained rows + sum(max_attempts - attempt) for READY/RUNNING tasks + the genuine prospective producer's maxAttempts. Actual attempt rows are checked against the task counter and distinct range. Terminal history contributes its actual rows only. RECONCILING cannot be claimed by JdbcTaskQueue (only READY and expired RUNNING can be claimed); it contributes actual attempts, with no invented future ordinary claims. All currently supported producers retain their original maxAttempts=3.
+- durable_task: distinct actual merged tasks + one only for a genuinely new producer. generation_input similarly adds the new input only if absent.
+- each minutes operation/execution/candidate: actual declared rows + one missing future row per nonterminal minutes task; a newly admitted minutes producer reserves one of each. Existing rows consume that reservation rather than being counted again. Terminal rows are retained when declared, without reserving nonexistent future effects. The total retained generation task bound also bounds its one-candidate history; selection can add its existing decided candidate without creating another producer footprint.
+- imported source tasks add only their actual local task/attempt growth, never a fictitious external operation/execution/candidate. Other producer kinds are not guessed into this rule.
+
+The one-operation/one-execution rule is specific to the actual minutes protocol: `MinutesTaskDomainPolicy.prepare` requires a single distinct spec and recomputes its exact input/fence-bound producer; `shape` fixes the genuine original protocol; the bound execution checks require executionNo=1. Generic Task7 retry requires old/new same input/fence/fingerprint. Under minutes, that fixes the same producer: the retry path can only encounter the already existing producer and cannot create a different task/execution; a changed producer is rejected by the domain gate. This is not a statement that generic Task7 or future Task11 tasks have one execution.
+
+The private policy owns the common minimum. Existing `MinutesTaskDomainPolicy.configure` merges registered immutable bounds by minimum; both genuine generation preparation and `MinutesEditorRuntime.compose` register with the same authority. Admission now calls that private policy's `reserveCapacity`, as claim/completion do, instead of using a potentially wider individual preparation instance. The test constructs tasks=2, then another preparation with tasks=256 and verifies it cannot widen admission. This is same-boot composition qualification, not hot-reload or live bound-change safety. Before any minutes registration, Task4 retains the actual fixed 256 shared selector bound; missing minutes processing configuration still rejects minutes task preparation. Formal new effects still require the real Task11 installation and remain failclosed.
+
+Real lock/check points:
+
+1. `JdbcMinutesGenerationPreparation.insert:76`: after genuine gate/current exact metadata capture and before generation input/task/business effects, reserve the new spec through the private policy. The queue's adopted domain remains the same issuer/holder. Existing receipt placeholder claim order remains the established idempotency protocol; a refusal rolls back it and leaves no committed receipt/audit/outbox/input/task.
+2. `MinutesTaskDomainPolicy.lockAll:48`: current capture under the same real root then the same occupied+remaining check before queue claim or completion/marker growth. Each new attempt consumes already reserved remaining capacity; it cannot first cross the bound on claim. All pre-existing legacy overflow is explicit.
+3. `JdbcProcessingIntake:72` carries the actual prepared import spec in `prepareMinutesIntake`; `JdbcTranscriptAuthority.lockMinutesTransitionTasks:239` verifies its prospective footprint under the same root after the complete domain/source/task lock sequence and before input/binding/task/business writes. No separate quota or issuer is supplied.
+4. `ImportTaskDomainPolicy:54,111,115,138,142` discovers the same complete declaration before identities, takes generation domain/candidate headers after transcript input/batch domain and before sources, then locks the merged task family and checks capacity. Both ordinary claim/completion and stale retirement retain the existing root and private prepared domain. Imports cannot enlarge attempts beyond the reserved footprint.
+5. Ordinary shared business transitions and decisions consume the same bounded declaration; source/minutes header union is compared in the established rank. Capacity does not authorize effects. Archives/hash/IO remain outside roots.
+
+Actual tasks=2 evidence admits the last generation after its one retained source task, rejects the next (including the wider 256 preparation) with exact input/task/receipt/audit/outbox equality, processes the accepted task through genuine local claim/FAILED, and permits ordinary shared CONFIRM. A separate actual Task4 HTTP case accepts the second import, runs the real worker to SUCCEEDED, refuses the third before committed input/binding/task/receipt/events, and preserves the accepted HTTP202 receipt replay. B03 and actual Task6 source concurrency/rank regression pass.
+
+Five exact-declaration tests cover the separate attempt selector: 84 active generation tasks + one historical source attempt + one new maxAttempts=3 producer = 256; the next admission fails. They also cover consumed-versus-remaining attempts, terminal and RECONCILING treatment, source-only local cost, PK deduplication, actual operation/execution/candidate reservation consumption, and 258-row legacy overflow without truncation. They do not claim a new 256-request remote capacity qualification.
